{% import "design/_example.html.jinja" as ex %}
{% import "design/components/_books_display_data.html.jinja" as demo %}
{% macro demos() %}
    <p class="ds-lead">
        A titled set of books, switchable between views. Give it a Solr <code>query</code> and it fetches and pages its own data and overlays the signed-in reader's shelf and rating; give it <code>books</code> to render a set you already have. Per-book actions go to <a href="#book-actions">Book Actions</a>. <code>view</code> is an open enum — <code>covers</code> (an <code>ol-carousel</code> of cover cards) and <code>list</code> (full-width rows) today, more layouts later without changing the data path. Templates should go through the <code>BooksDisplay</code> macro, which fills in the translated <code>labels</code>.
    </p>
    {% call ex.example("Covers view", "The default. Each card is a cover, a corner save button and the access CTA (Read / Borrow / Preview / Find in a library) the server derived from lending state. Title, year and author sit in the cover's hover card on pointer devices and under the cover on touch. With a query, nearing the end of the carousel loads the next page.",
        code='$:macros.BooksDisplay(query=\'subject_key:"fiction"\', title=_("Trending Books"), sort="trending", key="Demo")') %}
        <ol-books-display books='{{ demo.BOOKS|tojson }}' title="Trending Books" limit="12" url="/search?q=subject_key:fiction" analytics-key="DesignDemo" user-key="{{ user_key|default('', true) }}">
        </ol-books-display>
    {% endcall %}
    {% call ex.example("List view", "Rows add stars, a rating count and a split shelf button: the main part toggles the shown shelf, the chevron opens the actions popover. The footer pages with Show more / Collapse / See all.",
        code='$:macros.BooksDisplay(query=\'subject_key:"fiction"\', title=_("Trending Books"), view="list", key="Demo")') %}
        <ol-books-display view="list" books='{{ demo.BOOKS|tojson }}' title="Trending Books" limit="4" url="/search?q=subject_key:fiction" analytics-key="DesignDemo" user-key="{{ user_key|default('', true) }}">
        </ol-books-display>
    {% endcall %}
    <div class="ds-notes">
        <p>
            <b>Demo data.</b> Both examples pass a fixed <code>books</code> array — twelve real works covering every access CTA — so the gallery renders without a search index behind it; a real page passes <code>query</code>. Signed in, the demos are live: they overlay your own shelf and rating and write to your real reading log and lists.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Shadow DOM.</b> Styles ship in the component's <code>static styles</code> (class prefix <code>obd-</code>); only the pre-upgrade host box lives in <code>static/css/components/ol-books-display.css</code>. The two sitewide click delegations a shadow boundary defeats are handled inside the component: Matomo events are pushed by hand, and a logged-out CTA queues its own pending action instead of leaning on <code>.js-login-intent</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Signed out.</b> Without <code>user-key</code> the corner button and shelf controls send the visitor to log in, remembering the intent; no user-state request is made.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Labels.</b> Every string is a key in the <code>labels</code> JSON attribute with an English default; <code>%(name)s</code>-style placeholders match the server-side i18n strings.
        </p>
    </div>
{% endmacro %}
